1)Deadlock Debugging 

Cause of Deadlock:
P1 locks R1 and waits for R2.
P2 locks R2 and waits for R1.
Both processes wait for each other forever, causing circular wait (deadlock).
Optimization:
Lock resources in the same order for both processes (e.g., always lock R1 first, then R2).
This removes the circular wait condition and prevents deadlock.

2)CPU Utilization Problem 

Cause:
Processes repeatedly move between Ready and Waiting states.
They spend most of the time waiting for I/O operations, so the CPU remains idle.
System-Level Optimization:
Improve I/O performance (use faster storage or asynchronous I/O).
Use better CPU scheduling so another ready process runs while one waits for I/O.

3)Memory Bottleneck Debugging 

Cause:
The program experiences frequent page faults, meaning memory pages are constantly moved between RAM and disk.
Many threads increase memory usage and reduce performance.
Optimizations:
Reduce the number of threads or use a thread pool.
Optimize heap memory usage by freeing unused memory and reducing large allocations.

4)Starvation Debugging 

Root Cause:
P5 is in the lowest-priority queue (Q3).
Higher-priority queues (Q1 and Q2) keep getting CPU time, so P5 never executes.
Optimization:
Use aging, which gradually increases the priority of waiting processes.
This ensures every process eventually gets CPU time.

5)Context-Switch Overhead Debugging 

Cause:
The time quantum is only 1 ms, while the average burst time is 2 ms.
Frequent I/O waits and preemption cause many context switches.
Optimizations:
Increase the time quantum (e.g., from 1 ms to 4 ms).
Reduce unnecessary context switches by improving I/O handling (e.g., asynchronous I/O or batching requests).
